<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tadukhipa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 14th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tadukhipa was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> princess, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushratta">Tushratta</a>, who was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with a rich dowry to marry the ageing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> near the end of his reign. Her marriage and dowry are recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>. After Amenhotep's death she probably joined the household of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, and some scholars have identified her with his wife Kiya.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tadukhipa.jpg" alt="Tadukhipa" width="320" height="560" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CaptMondo · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AmarnaLetterOfMarriageNegotiation-BritishMuseum-August19-08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
